\section*{Question 6}

The Taylor series for a function \(f\) about \(x=1\) is given by \(\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}(-1)^{n+1} \frac{2^{n}}{n}(x-1)^{n}\) and converges to \(f(x)\) for \(|x-1|<R\), where \(R\) is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series.
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] Find the value of \(R\).
\item[(b)] Find the first three nonzero terms and the general term of the Taylor series for \(f^{\prime}\), the derivative of \(f\), about \(x=1\).
\item[(c)] The Taylor series for \(f^{\prime}\) about \(x=1\), found in part (b), is a geometric series. Find the function \(f^{\prime}\) to which the series converges for \(|x-1|<R\). Use this function to determine \(f\) for \(|x-1|<R\).
\item[(a)] Let \(a_{n}\) be the \(n\)th term of the Taylor series.
\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_{n}} & =\frac{(-1)^{n+2} 2^{n+1}(x-1)^{n+1}}{n+1} \cdot \frac{n}{(-1)^{n+1} 2^{n}(x-1)^{n}} \\
& =\frac{-2 n(x-1)}{n+1}
\end{aligned}
\]
\[
\begin{aligned}
& \lim _{n \rightarrow \infty}\left|\frac{-2 n(x-1)}{n+1}\right|=2|x-1| \\
& 2|x-1|<1 \Rightarrow|x-1|<\frac{1}{2}
\end{aligned}
\]
The radius of convergence is \(R=\frac{1}{2}\).
\item[(b)] The first three nonzero terms are
\[
2-4(x-1)+8(x-1)^{2} .
\]
The general term is \((-1)^{n+1} 2^{n}(x-1)^{n-1}\) for \(n \geq 1\).
\item[(c)] The common ratio is \(-2(x-1)\).
\[
\begin{aligned}
& f^{\prime}(x)=\frac{2}{1-(-2(x-1))}=\frac{2}{2 x-1} \text { for }|x-1|<\frac{1}{2} \\
& f(x)=\int \frac{2}{2 x-1} d x=\ln |2 x-1|+C
\end{aligned}
\]
\[
\begin{aligned}
& f(1)=0 \\
& \ln |1|+C=0 \Rightarrow C=0 \\
& f(x)=\ln |2 x-1| \text { for }|x-1|<\frac{1}{2}
\end{aligned}
\]
\item[] \(3:\left\{\begin{array}{l}1: \text { sets up ratio } \\ 1: \text { computes limit of ratio } \\ 1: \text { determines radius of convergence }\end{array}\right.\)
\item[] \(3:\left\{\begin{array}{l}2: \text { first three nonzero terms } \\ 1: \text { general term }\end{array}\right.\)
\item[] \(3:\left\{\begin{array}{l}1: f^{\prime}(x) \\ 1: \text { antiderivative } \\ 1: f(x)\end{array}\right.\)
\end{itemize}
